|~ What is declared under `::vec`: the refusal of its constructor, and
   the verbs of vectors, each with the parts of its object and its
   answer; a quote reads as the vector of its steps. Each verb is a name with the parts of its object, read and held
   to their laws before the primitive runs. ~|

:vec/construct
  |~~ Reads a vector as the bare vector, and a set, a quote or a doc as the vector of its elements. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/PayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::vec` reads a vector, a set or a quote as the bare vector; a
      payload of another kind is refused.
      ~(
        ::vec{:a 1}
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/PayloadNotVecError ::map]
       )
  ~~|

:vec/count
  |~~ The number of elements.

      ~([1 2 3] | count | eq 3)
      ~(42 | count !| /addresses | has ::vec/count)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/count/size
  |~~ How many elements the vector holds. ~~|
  ::number

:vec/empty
  |~~ Whether the vector holds no element.

      ~([] | empty | eq true)
      ~([1] | empty | eq false)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/empty/vacant
  |~~ Whether the vector holds no element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/first
  |~~ The first element, or null for the empty vector.

      ~([10 20 30] | first | eq 10)
      ~([] | first | eq null)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/last
  |~~ The last element, or null for the empty vector.

      ~([10 20 30] | last | eq 30)
      ~([] | last | eq null)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/sum
  |~~ The sum of the elements, each a number; the empty vector sums to 0.

      ~([1 2 3 4] | sum | eq 10)
      ~([] | sum | eq 0)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/sum/total
  |~~ The sum of the elements. ~~|
  ::number

:vec/sum/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `sum` adds numbers; `:index` names the first element that is
      none, and a field is read first, `* /amount | sum`.

      ~([1 "two" 3] | sum !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/sum/ElementNotNumberError ::string 1])
  ~~|

:vec/avg
  |~~ The mean of the elements, each a number, or null when there is none.

      ~([1 2 3 4] | avg | eq 2.5)
      ~([] | avg | eq null)
      ~([{:d :a :n 1} {:d :a :n 3} {:d :b :n 5}] | groupBy ~(/d) * (* /n | avg) | eq {:a 2 :b 5})
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/avg/mean
  |~~ The mean of the elements, null for an empty vector. ~~|
  #[::number ::null]

:vec/avg/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `avg` takes the mean of numbers; `:index` names the first element
      that is none, and a field is read first, `* /amount | avg`.

      ~([1 "two" 3] | avg !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/avg/ElementNotNumberError ::string 1])
  ~~|

:vec/min
  |~~ The first element in the one order of values, or the one whose key
      answers the first value, the key read as `sort` reads it. The
      element itself is the answer, the first of equal ones, and the
      empty vector yields null.

      ~([3 1 4 1 5] | min | eq 1)
      ~([{:age 30} {:age 20}] | min ~(/age) | eq {:age 20})
      ~([3 "a" null] | min | eq null)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/min/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/max
  |~~ The last element in the one order of values, or the one whose key
      answers the last value, the key read as `sort` reads it. The
      element itself is the answer, the first of equal ones, and the
      empty vector yields null.

      ~([3 1 4 1 5] | max | eq 5)
      ~([{:age 30} {:age 40}] | max ~(/age) | eq {:age 40})
      ~([3 "a" null] | max | eq "a")
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/max/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/filter
  |~~ The elements whose predicate answers true, in their order.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | filter ~(gt 2) | eq [3 4 5])
      The predicate reads each element as its subject:
      ~([{:age 25} {:age 15}] | filter ~(/age | gte 18) | eq [{:age 25}])
      A verb stands where a quote would:
      ~([1 -2 3] | :any/positive ~(gt 0) | filter ~positive | eq [1 3])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/filter/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean.

      The predicate of `filter` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `filter ~(gt 1)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | filter (count | gt 1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError ::boolean]
       )
      A step written without its quote runs on the whole subject at the
      call, and the error it answers is refused alike:
      ~(
        [{:a true}] | filter /a
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError ::proj/IndexNotIntegerError]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:vec/filter/kept
  |~~ The elements the predicate accepted, in order. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `filter` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.

      ~([1 2] | filter ~(1) !| [type /index /actualType] | eq [::vec/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])
  ~~|

:vec/every
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for every element, the question
      a law asks of a listing; it stops at the first false.

      ~([2 4 6 8] | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)
      ~([1 2 3] | every ~(gt 2) | eq false)
      A verb stands where a quote would:
      ~([2 4 6] | :any/positive ~(gt 0) | every ~positive | eq true)
      An empty container answers true:
      ~([] | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/every/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean.

      The predicate of `every` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `every ~(gt 0)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | every (count | gt 1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/every/predicate/NotQuoteError ::boolean]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/every/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:vec/every/all
  |~~ Whether the predicate held for every element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/every/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `every` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.

      ~([1 2] | every ~(1) !| [type /index /actualType] | eq [::vec/every/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])
  ~~|

:vec/any
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for some element; it stops at
      the first true.

      ~([1 2 3] | any ~(gt 2) | eq true)
      ~([1 2 3] | any ~(gt 99) | eq false)
      A verb stands where a quote would:
      ~([1 2 3] | :any/big ~(gt 2) | any ~big | eq true)
      An empty container answers false:
      ~([] | any ~(gt 0) | eq false)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/any/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean.

      The predicate of `any` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `any ~(gt 2)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | any (count | gt 1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/any/predicate/NotQuoteError ::boolean]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/any/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:vec/any/some
  |~~ Whether the predicate held for some element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/any/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `any` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.

      ~([1 2] | any ~(1) !| [type /index /actualType] | eq [::vec/any/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])
  ~~|

:vec/has
  |~~ Whether the vector holds the element, an element equal to it; the
      membership of a JSON array.

      ~(["ops" "db"] | has "ops" | eq true)
      ~([1 [2]] | has [2] | eq true)
      ~(42 | has :k !| /addresses | has ::map/has)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/has/element
  |~~ The element the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/has/held
  |~~ Whether the vector holds the element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/contains
  |~~ Whether the vector holds an element equal to the value, the
      membership `has` asks, under the word a string answers for its
      substrings.

      ~(["ops" "db"] | contains "ops" | eq true)
      ~(42 | contains "x" !| /addresses | eq #[::set/contains ::string/contains ::vec/contains])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/contains/element
  |~~ The element the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/contains/found
  |~~ Whether the vector holds the element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/groupBy
  |~~ The map of vectors the key sorts the elements into. A key of a
      map is a keyword, and a key that answers a string, the field of a
      JSON object, or a number files its element under the keyword of
      its text.

      ~([{:k :a :v 1} {:k :b :v 2} {:k :a :v 3}] | groupBy ~(/k) | /a * /v | eq [1 3])
      ~([{:s "paid"} {:s "open"} {:s "paid"}] | groupBy ~(/s) * count | eq {:paid 2 :open 1})

      Without a key an element files under itself, so the occurrences
      of each value are counted by `groupBy * count`:
      ~(["b" "a" "b"] | groupBy * count | eq {:b 2 :a 1})
      ~([{:dept :eng :name "a"} {:dept :sales :name "b"} {:dept :eng :name "c"}] | groupBy ~(/dept) | /eng * /name | eq ["a" "c"])
      ~([{:k 1} {:k 2} {:k 1}] | groupBy ~(/k) | keys | eq #[:"1" :"2"])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/groupBy/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none.

      The key of `groupBy` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `groupBy ~(/kind)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [{:kind :a}] | groupBy :a
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/groupBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError ::keyword]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/groupBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/groupBy/groups
  |~~ The elements filed under the keyword their key answered. ~~|
  ::map

:vec/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key `groupBy` files an element under is a keyword, or a string
      or a number, filed under the keyword of its text, since the keys of
      a map are keywords; any other value is refused, and `:index` names
      the element.

      ~([{:k true} {:k 2}] | groupBy ~(/k) !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError ::boolean 0])
  ~~|

:vec/indexBy
  |~~ The map of the elements under the key each answers, the last
      element winning a key two share; a key that answers a string is
      made a keyword first, as for `groupBy`.

      ~([{:id :a :n 1} {:id :b :n 2}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /b/n | eq 2)
      ~(["a" "b"] | indexBy | eq {:a "a" :b "b"})
      ~([{:id :a :name "alice"} {:id :b :name "bob"}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /a/name | eq "alice")
      ~([{:id 7 :name "ann"}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /7/name | eq "ann")
      ~([:x :y] | indexBy | eq {:x :x :y :y})
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/indexBy/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none.

      The key of `indexBy` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `indexBy ~(/id)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [{:id :a}] | indexBy :a
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/indexBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError ::keyword]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/indexBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/indexBy/index
  |~~ Each element under the keyword its key answered, the last of alike ones. ~~|
  ::map

:vec/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key `indexBy` files an element under is a keyword, or a string
      or a number, filed under the keyword of its text, since the keys of
      a map are keywords; any other value is refused, and `:index` names
      the element.

      ~([{:k [1]}] | indexBy ~(/k) !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError ::vec 0])
  ~~|

:vec/sort
  |~~ The elements in the one order of values, the order every value
      takes against every other, the way a reader ranks what a query
      found. `sort ~(key)` ranks by the value the key answers for each
      element, a vector it answers ranking place by place, so `sort
      ~([(/count | desc) /name])` reads as `ORDER BY count DESC, name`;
      elements the key leaves alike keep the order of the subject.

      Numbers ascend, equal ones side by side:
      ~([3 1 4 1 5] | sort | eq [1 1 3 4 5])
      A key orders by what it answers for each element:
      ~([{:age 30} {:age 20}] | sort ~(/age) | eq [{:age 20} {:age 30}])
      A key under `desc` descends, and a vector key breaks the ties by its
      next place:
      ~([{:age 30} {:age 40}] | sort ~(/age | desc) | first | /age | eq 40)
      ~([{:n 1 :s "b"} {:n 2 :s "c"} {:n 2 :s "a"}] | sort ~([(/n | desc) /s]) * /s | eq ["a" "c" "b"])
      Kinds come first, null, boolean, number, string, keyword, tag name, vector, set, map, quote, doc, error, elision and desc, then every other tag by its name:
      ~([3 null "x" 1] | sort | eq [null 1 3 "x"])
      ~([::B ::A] | sort | eq [::A ::B])
      A vector key ranks by its first place, then by the next among the elements the first leaves alike:
      ~([{:a 1 :b 2} {:a 1 :b 1} {:a 0 :b 9}] | sort ~([/a /b]) * /b | eq [9 1 2])
      ~([3 null 1] | sort ~([(eq null) /]) | eq [1 3 null])
      `desc` turns the key, or one place of it, to descend:
      ~([{:k 1} {:k 2}] | sort ~(/k | desc) | eq [{:k 2} {:k 1}])
      A key that answers an error answers the call, a place of a vector it answers among them, so a key that misses its field is refused where it is read:
      ~([{:a 2} {:b 1}] | sort ~(/a) !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)
      ~([{:a 2} {:b 1}] | sort ~([/a]) !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/sort/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none.

      The key of `sort` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `sort ~(/age)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [3 1 2] | sort 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/sort/sorted
  |~~ The elements in the order the key ranks them. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/take
  |~~ The first count elements. The count is a whole number: past the
      length the whole vector comes back, and a negative count takes
      nothing.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | take 3 | eq [1 2 3])
      ~([1 2 3] | take -1 | eq [])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/take/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/take/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:vec/take/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:vec/take/taken
  |~~ The first elements. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/drop
  |~~ The elements after the first count. The count is a whole number:
      past the length the empty vector comes back, and a negative count
      drops nothing.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | drop 2 | eq [3 4 5])
      ~([1 2 3] | drop -1 | eq [1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/drop/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/drop/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:vec/drop/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:vec/drop/rest
  |~~ The elements after the ones dropped. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/at
  |~~ The element at the index, a negative one counting from the end,
      or past either end the default the call gives, null when it gives
      none; an element that is null stays null.

      ~([10 20 30] | at 1 | eq 20)
      ~([10 20 30] | at -1 | eq 30)
      ~([] | at 0 7 | eq 7)
      ~([null] | at 0 7 | eq null)
      ~([10 20 30] | at 99 | eq null)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/at/default
  |~~ The value answered for a place the subject does not hold, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 1)

:vec/at/index
  |~~ The index, a negative one counting from the end. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/at/index/NotNumberError
  |~~ The index is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:vec/at/index/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The index is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:vec/distinct
  |~~ The set of the elements, the vector in the one order without
      duplicates. Two elements are one when `eq` says so, so two maps,
      vectors or sets of the same content collapse.

      ~([2 1 2 3 1] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])
      ~([2 1 2 3 1] | distinct | payload | eq [1 2 3])
      ~([{:id 1} {:id 2} {:id 1}] | distinct | eq #[{:id 1} {:id 2}])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/distinct/unique
  |~~ The elements, each once, in the one order: a set. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/reverse
  |~~ The elements in the reverse order.

      ~([1 2 3] | reverse | eq [3 2 1])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/reverse/reversed
  |~~ The elements in reverse order. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/flat
  |~~ The elements with one level of nesting spliced in.

      ~([[1 2] [3] [4 5]] | flat | eq [1 2 3 4 5])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/flat/flattened
  |~~ The elements with one level of nesting spliced in. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/reduce
  |~~ The seed folded over the elements by the reducer, which runs as
      `accumulator | reducer element`; `sum`, `count`, `max` and the
      builders of structures all factor through it.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 15)
      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 1 ~(mul) | eq 120)
      ~(["a" "b" "c"] | reduce "" ~(append) | eq "abc")
      ~([#[1] #[2 3]] | reduce #[] ~(union) | eq #[1 2 3])
      A name takes the accumulator as its subject and the element as the first part of its object:
      ~(:number/max2 ~(if (gt x) ~(/) ~(x)) | :number/max2/x |~~ The element. ::holds~(is ::number) ~~| ~(/0) | [3 1 4 1 5] | reduce 0 ~max2 | eq 5)
      ~(:any/tally ~(add 1) | :any/tally/el |~~ The element, unread. ~~| ~(/0) | [10 20 30] | reduce 0 ~tally | eq 3)
      The seed is the first accumulator, answered as it is for an empty subject:
      ~([] | reduce 42 ~(add) | eq 42)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/reduce/reducer
  |~~ The code that folds an element into the value so far.

      The reducer of `reduce` is code it runs on the accumulator with each element, so it takes a
      quote, `reduce 0 ~(add)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | reduce 0 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(/1)

:vec/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The reducer is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:vec/reduce/seed
  |~~ The value the fold starts from. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError
  |~~ The quote of `reduce` names one verb, which the fold calls with the
      accumulator as its subject and the element as the first part of its
      object, `reduce 0 ~(add)`; a quote of several steps, a literal or a
      name of no verb is refused. A fold of several steps is a name
      declared first, whose part names the element.

      ~([1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(mul 2 | add) !| type | eq ::vec/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError)
      ~(
        :any/step ~(mul 2 | add el) | :any/step/el ~(/0) | :any/step/el/NotNumberError ::holds~(is ::number)
        | [1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(step)
        | eq 11
       )
  ~~|

:vec/union
  |~~ The union of the operands the vector holds, sets merged in the one
      order or maps merged with the last value winning a key. A vector is
      no set for the algebra, so another operand beside it is
      refused with the pair.

      ~([#[:a :b] #[:b :c]] | union | eq #[:a :b :c])
      ~([1] | union #[2] !| type | eq ::vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/union/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/union/with/NotSetOrMapOrNullError
  |~~ The with is a set, a map or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::set ::map ::null)

:vec/union/NoOperandsError
  |~~ `union` over a vector joins its operands from the first, `[a b c]
      | union`, so the vector holds one at least; two operands are
      written `a | union b`.

      ~([] | union !| type | eq ::vec/union/NoOperandsError)
  ~~|

:vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError
  |~~ The operands `union` joins are two sets or two maps; a vector is
      none, so an operand its call gives beside the vector is refused with
      the pair.

      ~([#[:a] {:b 1}] | union !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError ::set ::map])
      ~([1] | union #[2] !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError ::vec ::set])
  ~~|

:vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:vec/minus
  |~~ The first operand the vector holds minus each one after it; another
      operand beside the vector is refused with the pair.

      ~([#[:a :b :c] #[:b]] | minus | eq #[:a :c])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/minus/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/minus/with/NotSetOrMapOrNullError
  |~~ The with is a set, a map or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::set ::map ::null)

:vec/minus/NoOperandsError
  |~~ `minus` over a vector joins its operands from the first, `[a b c]
      | minus`, so the vector holds one at least; two operands are
      written `a | minus b`.

      ~([] | minus !| type | eq ::vec/minus/NoOperandsError)
  ~~|

:vec/minus/OperandsMismatchedError
  |~~ The operands `minus` joins are two sets, or a map and a map or a
      vector of keys; a vector is none, so an operand its call gives beside
      the vector is refused with the pair.

      ~([#[:a] {:b 1}] | minus !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::vec/minus/OperandsMismatchedError ::set ::map])
      ~([1] | minus #[2] !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::vec/minus/OperandsMismatchedError ::vec ::set])
  ~~|

:vec/minus/OperandsMismatchedError/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:vec/minus/OperandsMismatchedError/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:vec/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ A key `minus` takes out of a map is a keyword or a string; `:index`
      names the first element of the vector of keys that is neither.

      ~([{:a 1} [:a 1]] | minus !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::number 1])
  ~~|

:vec/inter
  |~~ The intersection of the operands the vector holds; another operand
      beside the vector is refused with the pair.

      ~([#[:a :b :c] #[:b :d]] | inter | eq #[:b])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/inter/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/inter/with/NotSetOrMapOrNullError
  |~~ The with is a set, a map or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::set ::map ::null)

:vec/inter/NoOperandsError
  |~~ `inter` over a vector joins its operands from the first, `[a b c]
      | inter`, so the vector holds one at least; two operands are
      written `a | inter b`.

      ~([] | inter !| type | eq ::vec/inter/NoOperandsError)
  ~~|

:vec/inter/OperandsMismatchedError
  |~~ The operands `inter` joins are two sets, or a map and a map or a
      vector of keys; a vector is none, so an operand its call gives beside
      the vector is refused with the pair.

      ~([#[:a] {:b 1}] | inter !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::vec/inter/OperandsMismatchedError ::set ::map])
      ~([1] | inter #[2] !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::vec/inter/OperandsMismatchedError ::vec ::set])
  ~~|

:vec/inter/OperandsMismatchedError/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:vec/inter/OperandsMismatchedError/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:vec/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ A key `inter` keeps of a map is a keyword or a string; `:index`
      names the first element of the vector of keys that is neither.

      ~([{:a 1} [[:a]]] | inter !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::vec 0])
  ~~|

:vec/join
  |~~ The strings the vector holds joined with the separator between
      consecutive ones. Inverse of `split`.

      ~(["a" "b" "c"] | join "," | eq "a,b,c")
      ~([] | join "," | eq "")

      The subject of `join` is the vector of strings it writes as one; a
      map's values are read first, `vals | join ","`.
      ~(
        42 | join ","
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/join/SubjectNotVecError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/join/separator
  |~~ The separator the call gives.

      The separator of `join` is a string, `join ", "`.
      ~(
        ["a" "b"] | join 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/join/separator/NotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/join/separator/NotStringError
  |~~ The separator is a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:vec/join/joined
  |~~ The elements joined into one string by the separator. ~~|
  ::string

:vec/join/ElementNotStringError
  |~~ Every element `join` writes is a string; `:index` names the first
      that is not, and `* print` makes each value its text first.
      ~(
        ["a" 42 "c"] | join ","
        !| [type /actualType /index]
        |  eq [::vec/join/ElementNotStringError ::number 1]
       )
  ~~|

:vec/prepend
  |~~ The vector with the element at its head, any value; a set takes it
      through its constructor, so it stays a set.

      ~([2 3] | prepend 1 | eq [1 2 3])
      ~(#[2 3] | prepend 3 | eq #[2 3])
      ~(42 | prepend "x" !| /addresses | eq #[::string/prepend ::vec/prepend])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/prepend/element
  |~~ The element the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/prepend/prepended
  |~~ The vector with the value as its first element. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/append
  |~~ The vector with the element at its end, any value, so `[1] |
      append [2 3]` answers `[1 [2 3]]` and vectors join through
      `flat`; a set takes it through its constructor, so it stays a set.

      ~([1 2] | append 3 | eq [1 2 3])
      ~(#[1 2] | append 1 | eq #[1 2])
      ~(42 | append "x" !| /addresses | eq #[::string/append ::vec/append])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/append/element
  |~~ The element the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/append/appended
  |~~ The vector with the value as its last element. ~~|
  ::vec
